WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
A bacterium gained a useful gene.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.300
What carried it there, and can that journey reveal a map?

3
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.780
Watch the donor DNA, shown in gold.

4
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.700
Bacteria do not make meiotic crosses, yet donor genes can become inherited.

5
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.490
One path exposes free DNA for transformation.

6
00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:27.070
Another joins donor and recipient for conjugation.

7
00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:32.490
The third protects donor DNA inside a phage during transduction.

8
00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.890
Competent cells take up the free DNA.

9
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.470
A filter that stops cells blocks only the contact path.

10
00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:46.990
DNase destroys exposed DNA, but a phage coat protects its cargo.

11
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.510
That two-step test turns three pictures into a decision.

12
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:57.510
An unknown transfer survives the enzyme, but stops when cells are separated.

13
00:00:58.910 --> 00:00:59.910
Which carrier fits?

14
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:02.830
Follow both clues before choosing.

15
00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:05.990
One result rules out free DNA.

16
00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:08.790
The other rules out a phage.

17
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:21.177
Conjugation fits. Its DNA is never exposed, so the enzyme misses it.

18
00:01:22.397 --> 00:01:25.217
Separating the cells removes the connection it needs.

19
00:01:26.457 --> 00:01:31.077
Both clues point to direct contact, not merely DNA moving through the medium.

20
00:01:32.117 --> 00:01:35.677
In E. coli, the F factor is a small DNA circle.

21
00:01:36.877 --> 00:01:39.777
Free F transfers itself from a donor to a recipient.

22
00:01:41.137 --> 00:01:45.977
One strand crosses from F+ plus to F−, minus, and each cell completes the circle.

23
00:01:47.457 --> 00:01:49.197
Chromosomal markers rarely follow.

24
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.090
When F joins the donor chromosome, the cell is Hfr.

25
00:01:55.570 --> 00:02:00.150
Transfer now pulls chromosomal markers through the same origin in a fixed order.

26
00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:05.850
Because pairs usually separate early, recipients receive only part of the

27
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:09.830
chromosome. The remaining F sequence would arrive last.

28
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:15.053
Break different pairs apart at different times, then score the recipients.

29
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:18.553
Markers nearer the origin appear first.

30
00:02:19.753 --> 00:02:22.993
With steady transfer, the time gaps become map distances.

31
00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:28.233
Selective plates reveal which recipients inherited each marker.

32
00:02:29.513 --> 00:02:33.733
A marker's entry time is the earliest interruption that still yields it.

33
00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:39.377
A different Hfr origin can send the same chromosome the other way.

34
00:02:40.957 --> 00:02:45.177
Absolute arrival times change, but matching gaps reveal one circular map.

35
00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:51.577
The origin sets the starting time, so absolute times cannot be compared directly

36
00:02:51.577 --> 00:02:52.577
across donors.

37
00:02:53.617 --> 00:02:58.517
Selection makes rare recipients visible, but a colony reports more than arrival.

38
00:02:59.877 --> 00:03:03.617
The donor piece must also be expressed and usually integrated.

39
00:03:04.977 --> 00:03:08.597
Incoming linear DNA cannot copy itself with the chromosome.

40
00:03:10.157 --> 00:03:13.417
Recombination can replace the matching recipient segment.

41
00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:16.307
The route created the readout.

42
00:03:17.527 --> 00:03:21.047
For Hfr transfer, ordered arrival maps gene order.

43
00:03:22.347 --> 00:03:26.127
If transfer is roughly steady, time gaps also map distance.

44
00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:29.607
Keep the gaps, not the clock's starting values.
